@database "sqlite_query"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: sqlite_query.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "SQLite query"
@{h1}SQLite query

Gives an agent raw SQL on one SQLite database file: it can look at the
tables, count and join, and -- because the plugin exists to repair data as
well as inspect it -- insert, update, delete and change the schema. Every
statement the model writes is executed and committed; there is no dry run
and no backup. What the plugin does guard is the boundary: a statement
stays inside the one configured file, stops after a time limit or when the
run is stopped, and returns a bounded number of rows. It has no panel and
is off in the default configuration.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Running SQL  " link tool}
  @{"  Security and limits  " link security}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node tool "Running SQL"
@{h1}Running SQL

@{tt}sqlite_query_execute_sql@{utt} (the name follows the plugin instance)
runs one SQL statement on the instance's database and answers with its rows
or with what it changed.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
sql | required; exactly one statement. A second statement after a semicolon is refused, not ignored.
@{body}

There are no bound parameters: the model writes the values into the SQL.

@{h2}What runs

@{bullet}Every call opens its own connection and closes it at the end. A
statement that changes data is committed before the answer is sent -- also
@{tt}INSERT@{utt}, @{tt}UPDATE@{utt} and @{tt}DELETE@{utt} with
@{tt}RETURNING@{utt}, whose rows come back like a query's.
@{bullet}A transaction cannot span calls: @{tt}BEGIN@{utt} in one call is
committed, empty, at its end.
@{bullet}A statement that fails, runs out of time or is stopped leaves the
database as it was.
@{bullet}@{tt}PRAGMA@{utt} works for reading (@{tt}PRAGMA table_info(t)@{utt})
and for settings of the file (@{tt}journal_mode@{utt} and the like); the
two pragmas that point SQLite at a directory are refused, see
@{" Security and limits " link security}.

@{h2}Answers

A statement that returns rows -- @{tt}SELECT@{utt}, most @{tt}PRAGMA@{utt}s,
anything with @{tt}RETURNING@{utt} -- answers with:

@{table}
Field | Meaning
status | success
columns | the column names in order; a name that occurs twice gets a suffix (id, id_2)
rows | one object per row, column name to value; long text and BLOBs as in @{" What the model sees " link model}
row_count | how many rows are in this answer
truncated | true when the statement had more than max_rows (500) rows; absent otherwise
hint | with truncated: "only the first 500 rows; use LIMIT/OFFSET for more"
@{body}

Any other statement answers with @{tt}status@{utt},
@{tt}rows_affected@{utt} -- the rows the statement itself inserted, changed
or deleted, not those its triggers touched; 0 for schema changes -- and
@{tt}last_row_id@{utt}, the rowid of the last row an @{tt}INSERT@{utt} made,
else null.

@{h2}Errors

Every error answer has @{tt}status@{utt} error and @{tt}error@{utt}:

@{table}
error | When
sql parameter is required | sql is missing or empty
Database not found: <path> | the configured file does not exist; the plugin never creates it
You can only execute one statement at a time. | more than one statement
not authorized | ATTACH, VACUUM INTO, a directory pragma, setting a heap limit, or load_extension()
interrupted after query_timeout (30 s) | the statement ran out of time
no such table / no such column | a name miss; see below
string or blob too big | a value larger than max_value_bytes (64 MB) would be made, stored or read
any other SQLite message | syntax errors, constraints, a locked database ("database is locked" after query_timeout seconds of waiting)
@{body}

When the run is stopped while a statement is running, the answer is
@{tt}{"error": "Tool 'execute_sql' was cancelled.", "cancelled": true}@{utt}
(plus @{tt}forced@{utt}).

@{h2}Name misses

For @{tt}no such table@{utt} and @{tt}no such column@{utt} the error carries
the query the model would have typed next:

@{bullet}a missing table: @{tt}tables@{utt} (up to 25 tables and views of
the file), @{tt}did_you_mean@{utt} (the closest name, or null) and a
@{tt}hint@{utt};
@{bullet}a missing column: @{tt}columns@{utt}, the columns (up to 30 each)
of the tables whose names appear as words in the SQL -- of all tables, up
to 25, when none does -- with @{tt}did_you_mean@{utt} and a @{tt}hint@{utt}.

Other errors carry no guess.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"sql": "SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS n FROM items GROUP BY status"}
{"sql": "UPDATE items SET status = 'done' WHERE id = 7"}
@{body}

@{code json}
{"status": "success", "rows": [{"status": "done", "n": 12}, {"status": "draft", "n": 3}],
 "row_count": 2, "columns": ["status", "n"]}
{"status": "success", "rows_affected": 1, "last_row_id": null}
@{body}
@endnode

@node security "Security and limits"
@{h1}Security and limits

The model writes the SQL, and anything it has read -- a web page, a file, a
row in this very database -- can suggest what to write. Inside the file the
plugin trusts it completely; the limits below are about everything outside.

@{h2}Inside the file: everything

There is no read-only mode and no statement classification. The model can
@{tt}DROP@{utt} a table, run @{tt}UPDATE@{utt} without @{tt}WHERE@{utt},
rewrite the schema. Give the tool only to an agent that may change this
data, and point the instance at a copy when it only needs to look.

@{h2}Outside the file: nothing

The database path comes from the server settings, never from the model. An
authorizer on every connection refuses what would reach another path:

@{bullet}@{tt}ATTACH@{utt} -- it would read any SQLite file the process can
open, and create a new file anywhere it can write, network shares
(@{tt}\\\\host\\share\\x.db@{utt}) included.
@{bullet}@{tt}VACUUM INTO@{utt} -- it writes a copy of the database to any
path.
@{bullet}@{tt}PRAGMA temp_store_directory@{utt} and
@{tt}data_store_directory@{utt} -- they move where SQLite puts files, for
the whole process.
@{bullet}Setting @{tt}PRAGMA hard_heap_limit@{utt} or
@{tt}soft_heap_limit@{utt} -- the limit holds for every SQLite connection
in the process and can only be lowered, so one call could starve all the
others. Reading them is allowed.

Also closed, without the authorizer's help: @{tt}load_extension()@{utt}
answers "not authorized" (Python never enables extension loading), and
file functions such as @{tt}readfile()@{utt} and @{tt}writefile()@{utt} do
not exist in the SQLite Python ships ("no such function"). Besides the
database file, SQLite itself writes only its own journal next to it
(@{tt}-journal@{utt}, or @{tt}-wal@{utt} and @{tt}-shm@{utt} in WAL mode)
and temporary files in the system's temp directory.

@{h2}Time

@{tt}query_timeout@{utt} (30 seconds) is two limits at once:

@{bullet}how long the call waits for a lock another connection holds; then
it answers "database is locked";
@{bullet}how long the call may take in all, counted from its start: SQLite
looks at the clock every 10,000 steps and stops the statement -- a
recursive query that never ends, a cross join of large tables. The answer
is "interrupted after query_timeout (30 s)", and whatever the statement had
changed is rolled back.

When the person stops the run, the statement is stopped at the next such
look too, instead of running on in the background after the tool call is
abandoned.

@{h2}Size

@{bullet}At most @{tt}max_rows@{utt} (500) rows reach the answer; SQLite
does not even produce the rest. The answer says @{tt}truncated@{utt}.
@{bullet}No single value may exceed @{tt}max_value_bytes@{utt} (64 MB).
SQLite builds a value in one step, out of reach of the time limit:
without this cap @{tt}SELECT zeroblob(900000000)@{utt} took 13.5 GB of
memory. The cap holds for everything the call makes, stores or reads: a
value already in the file above it can no longer be read through this
tool at all, not even with @{tt}substr()@{utt} or @{tt}length()@{utt} --
raise the setting for such a file.
@{bullet}In the answer, a text longer than @{tt}max_value_chars@{utt}
(10,000) is cut and ends in @{tt}…[<length> chars]@{utt}; ask for
@{tt}substr(col, 10001, 10000)@{utt} to read on.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tool

The tool and its one parameter are described in the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}: raw SQL on the configured database, full access, no
backups, one statement, with three example statements. The description
does not name the database, its tables or the limits; the model learns the
tables from a query (@{tt}sqlite_master@{utt}) or from a name miss, and the
row cap from a truncated answer.

@{h2}The status line

While the statement runs the chat shows "Executing: " and its first 50
characters. It ends with "Query returned 12 row(s)", "Query returned 500
row(s), more cut off", "Query affected 1 row(s)", or the error: "SQL error:
no such column: titel -- did you mean 'title'?", "Database not found:
<path>", "Stopped on cancel: <sql>".

@{h2}Values

The answer reaches the model as JSON. Text, numbers and NULL arrive as
they are, text longer than @{tt}max_value_chars@{utt} cut to
@{tt}abc…[52000 chars]@{utt}. A BLOB has no JSON form; it arrives as an SQL
hex literal, @{tt}x'00ff'@{utt}, which the model can paste back into a
statement. One of more than half of @{tt}max_value_chars@{utt} bytes is cut
the same way: @{tt}x'89504e47'…[40000 bytes]@{utt}.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Answers are ordinary tool results, appended; nothing earlier changes. A
query answer is bounded by @{tt}max_rows@{utt} rows of values of at most
@{tt}max_value_chars@{utt} each -- still 500 rows of several wide text
columns make a large answer. Select the columns you need.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}One database per instance. For a second file, configure a second
instance under another name; its tool is named after it.
@{bullet}Everything inside the file is allowed; there is no confirmation
step for destructive statements.
@{bullet}The column recovery finds the tables of a query by their names as
words in the SQL; a table name with spaces is not recognised that way, and
the recovery lists the columns of all tables instead.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is off in the default configuration: @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}
carries its entry commented out, to be switched on for a particular
investigation. An entry looks like this:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    sqlite_query:
      type: sqlite_query
      enabled: true
      database: "data/app.db"
      query_timeout: 30
@{body}

An agent sees the tool when its allowlist names it:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+sqlite_query/*"
@{body}

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
database | default.db | the SQLite file; a relative path counts from the folder the server was started in. It must exist.
query_timeout | 30 | seconds: the lock wait and the time limit of one call
max_rows | 500 | the most rows one answer carries
max_value_bytes | 67108864 (64 MB) | the largest value SQLite may make, store or read in a call
max_value_chars | 10000 | longer text in an answer is cut; BLOBs at half as many bytes
@{body}

A missing, zero, negative or non-numeric value for the last three means
the default.

For two databases, add two instances with their own names
(@{tt}orders_db: {type: sqlite_query, database: ...}@{utt}); each has its
own tool, @{tt}orders_db_execute_sql@{utt}. The plugin stores nothing of
its own.

@{h2}From the command line

The same tool runs without the API, as the installed command or as a
module (with @{tt}src@{utt} on the Python path):

@{code bash}
tool-sqlite-query --database data/app.db --sql "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM items"
python -m plugins.sqlite_query -d data/app.db -s "PRAGMA table_info(items)" --json
@{body}

It prints a table (or the JSON answer with @{tt}--json@{utt}), the
suggestion on a name miss, and exits with 1 when the statement failed. It
uses the defaults of all settings except @{tt}database@{utt}, and
writes to the file like the tool does.

The plugin needs nothing beyond Python's own @{tt}sqlite3@{utt}.
@endnode
